what is HTML. HTML stands for Hyper Text Markup Language. HTML is the standard language for web pages. HTML tells the browser how to display content. HTML uses tags. Tags are written like this <tagname>. Most tags come in pairs. Opening tag is like <p>. Closing tag is like </p>. The content goes between the tags.

how to write basic HTML. Start with !DOCTYPE html. Then open html tag. Then head tag. Then title tag. Then body tag. Here is a simple example. html head title My Page title head body h1 Hello World h1 p This is a paragraph p body html.

what is an HTML element. An HTML element starts with a start tag. Then content. Then an end tag. For example h1 Heading h1 is an element. Some elements are empty like br and hr. They have no closing tag.

what are headings in HTML. HTML has six heading tags. h1 is the biggest. h2 is smaller than h1. h3 is smaller than h2. h4 is smaller than h3. h5 is smaller than h4. h6 is the smallest. Search engines use headings to understand page structure.

what is a paragraph tag. The paragraph tag is p. It creates a paragraph of text. Browsers add space before and after each paragraph. Example p This is a paragraph p.

what is a link tag. Link is created using a tag. a stands for anchor. It needs href attribute. href tells where to go. Example a href https google com Visit Google a. The link text is between a and a.

what is an image tag. Image tag is img. It is an empty tag. No closing tag needed. It needs src attribute. src tells where image is. It also needs alt attribute. alt describes the image. Example img src photo jpg alt my photo.

what are HTML lists. There are two types of lists. Ordered lists use ol tag. Unordered lists use ul tag. List items use li tag. Ordered lists have numbers. Unordered lists have bullets. Example ul li item one li li item two li ul.

what is a div tag. Div is a container. It groups other elements together. It is like a box. Div has no special meaning. It is used for styling and layout. Example div class container p inside div p div.

what is a span tag. Span is like div but inline. It does not create a new line. It is used for small parts of text. Example p My span color red name span is here p.

what are HTML attributes. Attributes give extra info about elements. They come in name value pairs. Example p class intro This is a paragraph p. class is attribute name. intro is attribute value. id is another attribute. src is for images. href is for links.

how to make a button. Button tag is button. It creates a clickable button. Text goes between button tags. Example button Click Me button. Button can have onclick attribute. onclick runs JavaScript when clicked.

what is a form in HTML. Form tag is form. It collects user input. Input fields go inside form. Input tag is input. Input needs type attribute. Type text is for text input. Type submit is for submit button. Example form input type text name name input type submit form.

what is a table in HTML. Table tag is table. Tr tag makes table row. Th tag makes header cell. Td tag makes data cell. Example table tr th Name th th Age th tr tr td John td td 25 td tr table.

how to add CSS to HTML. CSS makes HTML look good. Inline CSS uses style attribute. Internal CSS uses style tag. External CSS uses link tag. Example p style color red Red text p.

what is class and id in HTML. Class and id are used for styling. Class can be used many times. Id must be unique. Class uses dot in CSS. Id uses hash in CSS. Example div class box div. div id main div.
HTML Lesson 1: What is HTML?
HTML stands for Hyper Text Markup Language. It is not a programming language. It is a markup language. HTML tells the web browser how to show content. Every webpage you see is made with HTML.
Example of basic HTML:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My First Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello World</h1>
    <p>This is my first webpage.</p>
</body>
</html>

HTML Lesson 2: HTML Tags
Tags are the building blocks of HTML. Tags are written with angle brackets. Most tags come in pairs. Opening tag is <tagname>. Closing tag is </tagname>. The content goes between them.
Example of tags:
<h1>This is a heading</h1>
<p>This is a paragraph</p>
<b>This text is bold</b>
<i>This text is italic</i>

HTML Lesson 3: Headings
Headings are important in HTML. There are 6 heading levels. h1 is most important. h6 is least important. Search engines use headings to understand your page.
Example of all headings:
<h1>Heading level 1</h1>
<h2>Heading level 2</h2>
<h3>Heading level 3</h3>
<h4>Heading level 4</h4>
<h5>Heading level 5</h5>
<h6>Heading level 6</h6>

HTML Lesson 4: Paragraphs
Paragraphs are for text content. Use the p tag. Browsers add space before and after each paragraph. Never use multiple spaces in HTML. Use multiple p tags instead.
Example of paragraphs:
<p>This is first paragraph.</p>
<p>This is second paragraph.</p>
<p>This is third paragraph with <b>bold text</b> inside.</p>

HTML Lesson 5: Links
Links let users click and go to other pages. Use the a tag. a stands for anchor. The href attribute tells where to go. The text between a tags is what users see.
Example of links:
<a href="https://www.google.com">Go to Google</a>
<a href="about.html">About Us</a>
<a href="contact.html">Contact Page</a>

HTML Lesson 6: Images
Images make webpages beautiful. Use the img tag. img is an empty tag. No closing tag needed. src attribute tells image location. alt attribute describes image for screen readers.
Example of images:
<img src="photo.jpg" alt="My photo">
<img src="logo.png" alt="Company logo">
<img src="https://example.com/pic.jpg" alt="Online image">

HTML Lesson 7: Lists
Lists organize information. Two types of lists. ul is unordered list with bullets. ol is ordered list with numbers. li is list item. Lists can be inside other lists.
Example of unordered list:
<ul>
    <li>Apple</li>
    <li>Banana</li>
    <li>Orange</li>
</ul>
Example of ordered list:
<ol>
    <li>Wake up</li>
    <li>Brush teeth</li>
    <li>Eat breakfast</li>
</ol>

HTML Lesson 8: Div and Span
Div is a container. It blocks the whole line. Span is also a container. It only takes needed space. Div is for big sections. Span is for small parts of text.
Example of div:
<div style="background:blue; color:white;">
    <h2>This is inside div</h2>
    <p>This paragraph too</p>
</div>
Example of span:
<p>My name is <span style="color:red;">Red</span> color.</p>

HTML Lesson 9: Buttons
Buttons let users click and do actions. Use the button tag. Text goes between button tags. Buttons can run JavaScript with onclick attribute.
Example of buttons:
<button>Click Me</button>
<button onclick="alert('Hello!')">Say Hello</button>
<button style="background:green; color:white;">Green Button</button>

HTML Lesson 10: Forms
Forms collect user input. Use the form tag. Input tags go inside form. Different input types do different things. Submit button sends the form.
Example of simple form:
<form>
    <label>Name:</label>
    <input type="text" placeholder="Enter name">
    <br>
    <label>Email:</label>
    <input type="email" placeholder="Enter email">
    <br>
    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>

HTML Lesson 11: Tables
Tables show data in rows and columns. Use table tag. tr makes a row. th makes a header cell. td makes a regular cell. Border shows table lines.
Example of table:
<table border="1">
    <tr>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Age</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>John</td>
        <td>25</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Sarah</td>
        <td>30</td>
    </tr>
</table>

HTML Lesson 12: Putting It All Together
Now let's make a complete webpage. This webpage uses heading, paragraph, link, image, list, button, form, and table.
Example complete webpage:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Complete Website</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    <p>This is my complete webpage with everything I learned.</p>
    
    <a href="about.html">About Me</a>
    
    <h2>My Favorite Things</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Coding</li>
        <li>Reading</li>
        <li>Music</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>Contact Me</h2>
    <form>
        <input type="text" placeholder="Your Name">
        <button type="submit">Send</button>
    </form>
    
    <footer>Copyright 2025</footer>
</body>
</html>
HTML Lesson 13: HTML Attributes Explained
Attributes give extra information to HTML tags. Attributes come in name="value" pairs. Every tag can have attributes. id attribute gives unique name to any element. class attribute groups similar elements. style attribute adds CSS directly. src attribute tells image path. href attribute tells link destination. alt attribute describes image. title attribute shows tooltip on hover.
Example of different attributes:
<p id="first-paragraph">This paragraph has unique ID</p>
<p class="text-content">This paragraph is in text class</p>
<p style="color:blue; font-size:20px;">Blue big text</p>
<img src="dog.jpg" alt="Cute dog" title="Dog photo">
<a href="https://google.com" title="Go to Google">Google</a>

HTML Lesson 14: Comment Your Code
Comments explain your code to humans. Browsers ignore comments. Comments help you remember what code does. Other developers can understand your code faster. Use <!-- comment here --> for comments.
Example of comments:
<!-- This is a heading for main section -->
<h1>Welcome</h1>
<!-- Below is the navigation menu -->
<nav>
    <a href="home.html">Home</a>
</nav>
<!-- TODO: Add more links later -->

HTML Lesson 15: Line Breaks and Horizontal Lines
br tag creates a line break. It is an empty tag. No closing tag needed. hr tag creates a horizontal line. hr is also empty tag. Use br to start new line. Use hr to separate sections.
Example of line breaks:
<p>First line<br>Second line<br>Third line</p>
<p>Address:<br>
123 Main Street<br>
New York, NY 10001</p>
Example of horizontal lines:
<h1>Section 1</h1>
<p>Content for section 1</p>
<hr>
<h1>Section 2</h1>
<p>Content for section 2</p>

HTML Lesson 16: Text Formatting Tags
HTML has many text formatting tags. b tag makes bold text. strong tag makes important bold text. i tag makes italic text. em tag makes emphasized italic text. small tag makes smaller text. mark tag highlights text. del tag shows deleted text. ins tag shows inserted text. sub tag makes subscript text. sup tag makes superscript text.
Example of all formatting:
<p><b>Bold text</b></p>
<p><strong>Important bold text</strong></p>
<p><i>Italic text</i></p>
<p><em>Emphasized text</em></p>
<p><small>Smaller text</small></p>
<p><mark>Highlighted text</mark></p>
<p><del>Deleted text</del></p>
<p><ins>Inserted text</ins></p>
<p>H<sub>2</sub>O is water</p>
<p>10<sup>th</sup> class</p>

HTML Lesson 17: Block vs Inline Elements
Block elements take full width. They start on new line. Inline elements take only needed width. They stay on same line. div is block. p is block. h1 to h6 are block. span is inline. a is inline. img is inline. button is inline. strong is inline. em is inline.
Example showing difference:
<div style="background:lightblue;">This div is block element</div>
<div style="background:lightgreen;">Another div on new line</div>
<span style="background:yellow;">Span is inline</span>
<span style="background:pink;">Same line as previous span</span>

HTML Lesson 18: Nested Elements
Elements can go inside other elements. This is called nesting. Proper nesting is important. Always close inner tags before outer tags. Indentation shows nesting level. Browsers understand nested structure.
Example of nested elements:
<div class="container">
    <div class="header">
        <h1>Website Title</h1>
        <p>Tagline goes here</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content">
        <ul>
            <li>First item</li>
            <li>Second item</li>
            <li>Third item</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

HTML Lesson 19: Building a Navigation Menu
Navigation helps users move around website. Use nav tag for navigation. Use ul and li for menu items. Use a tags for links. Active link shows current page.
Example of navigation menu:
<nav>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="index.html">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="about.html">About Us</a></li>
        <li><a href="services.html">Services</a></li>
        <li><a href="contact.html">Contact</a></li>
    </ul>
</nav>

HTML Lesson 20: Building a Card Layout
Cards are boxes that hold content. Cards use div with border and padding. Each card has heading, text, and button. Cards can be arranged in rows.
Example of a single card:
<div style="border:1px solid gray; padding:15px; width:300px;">
    <h3>Product Title</h3>
    <p>Product description goes here. This explains what the product does.</p>
    <button>Buy Now</button>
</div>

HTML Lesson 21: Building a Simple Blog Layout
Blog has header, posts, sidebar, and footer. Header has title and nav. Posts section has articles. Sidebar has categories. Footer has copyright.
Example blog layout:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Blog</title>
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        <h1>My Personal Blog</h1>
        <nav>
            <a href="index.html">Home</a>
            <a href="posts.html">Posts</a>
            <a href="about.html">About</a>
        </nav>
    </header>
    <main>
        <article>
            <h2>First Blog Post</h2>
            <p>Posted on January 1st, 2025</p>
            <p>This is my first blog post content. I am excited to start blogging.</p>
            <a href="post1.html">Read More</a>
        </article>
        <article>
            <h2>Second Blog Post</h2>
            <p>Posted on January 2nd, 2025</p>
            <p>This is my second post. I am learning HTML every day.</p>
            <a href="post2.html">Read More</a>
        </article>
    </main>
    <footer>
        <p>Copyright 2025 My Blog</p>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>

